{"abstract":"A bicycle node network is a wayfinding system targeted at recreational cyclists, consisting of numbered signposts placed alongside already existing infrastructure. Bicycle node networks are becoming increasingly popular as they encourage sustainable tourism and rural cycling, while also being flexible and cost-effective to implement. However, the lack of a formalized methodology and data-driven tools for the planning of such networks is a hindrance to their adaptation on a larger scale. To address this need, we present the BikeNodePlanner: A fully open-source decision support tool, consisting of modular Python scripts to be run in the free and open-source geographic information system QGIS. The BikeNodePlanner allows the user to evaluate and compare bicycle node network plans through a wide range of metrics, such as land use, proximity to points of interest, and elevation across the network. The BikeNodePlanner provides data-driven decision support for bicycle node network planning and can hence be of great use for regional planning, cycling tourism, and the promotion of rural cycling.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Vybornova, Anastassia","Vierø, Ane Rahbek","Krogh Hansen, Kirsten","Szell, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-830686"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Austria"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Complexity Science Hub"],"keyword":["Bicycle node networks","Cycling tourism","Rural cycling"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Danish Coastal and Rural Tourism"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-RUC"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-RUC"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"bikenodeplanner a data driven decision support tool for bicycle node network planning","src":["ruc"],"src-code":["DI-U-RUC"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-830686","identifier":[{"id":"105013514535","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ruc-b79f528e-08f7-4951-a789-c8ca67494c80"],"keyword":["Bicycle node networks","Cycling tourism","Rural cycling"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-75C8C2884B07FA28CDAC47203A615527"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"ruc roskilde university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-RUC","l3nl":"Roskilde University","l3nn":"RUC Roskilde University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"RUC Roskilde University","namevar":["Roskilde University, Department of People and Technology","Roskilde University, Department of People and Technology, Mobility, Space, Place and Urban Studies (MOSPUS)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-RUC","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"dk","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Danish Coastal and Rural Tourism"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"at","key":"complexity science hub","loc":["Austria","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Complexity Science Hub","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","first":"Anastassia","key":"vybornovapau","last":"Vybornova","name":"Vybornova, Anastassia","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","country":"dk","first":"Ane Rahbek","key":"vierøpau","last":"Vierø","locid":"68c94971-c40a-4223-af24-f2b476d7f7ba","name":"Vierø, Ane Rahbek","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Kirsten","key":"kroghhansenpau","last":"Krogh Hansen","name":"Krogh Hansen, Kirsten","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 5","first":"Michael","key":"szellpau","last":"Szell","name":"Szell, Michael","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/23998083251355999","issn":["23998083","23998091"],"issn_org":["23998083"],"issue":"7","journal":"Environment and Planning B: Urban Analytics and City Science","pages":"1771-1780","paperid":"23998083251355999","status":"p","title":"Environment and Planning B: Urban Analytics and City Science","volume":"52"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105013514535"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Roskilde University","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ruc-b79f528e-08f7-4951-a789-c8ca67494c80"],"src":["RUC Roskilde University"],"title":"BikeNodePlanner : A data-driven decision support tool for bicycle node network planning","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-830686","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Urban highways are common, especially in the United States, making cities more car-centric. They promise the annihilation of distance but obstruct pedestrian mobility, thus playing a key role in limiting social interactions locally. Although this limiting role is widely acknowledged in urban studies, the quantitative relationship between urban highways and social ties is barely tested. Here, we define a Barrier Score that relates massive, geolocated online social network data to highways in the 50 largest US cities. At the granularity of individual social ties, we show that urban highways are associated with decreased social connectivity. This barrier effect is especially strong for short distances and consistent with historical cases of highways that were built to purposefully disrupt or isolate Black neighborhoods. By combining spatial infrastructure with social tie data, our method adds a dimension to demographic studies of social segregation. Our study can inform reparative planning for an evidence-based reduction of spatial inequality, and more generally, support a better integration of the social fabric in urban planning.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Aiello, Luca Maria","Vybornova, Anastassia","Juhász, Sándor","Szell, Michael","Bokányi, Eszter"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-808930"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Hungary","Netherlands"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Corvinus University of Budapest","University of Amsterdam"],"keyword":["Highways","Segregation","Social media","Social network","Urban Computing"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"urban highways are barriers to social ties","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109113511/pnas25urban.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["L.M.A. acknowledges funding from Carlsberg Foundation Project COCOONS (Grant ID: CF210432). M.S. acknowledges funding from European Union Horizon Project JUST STREETS (Grant agreement ID: 101104240). S.J. acknowledges funding from EU Marie Skłodowska-Curie Postdoctoral Fellowship Programme (Grant number 101062606)"],"id":"S01-000.00-808930","identifier":[{"id":"86000234747","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-ff25188e-1a00-458c-9cc9-495a1baf903e"],"keyword":["Urban Computing","barrier effect","highways","segregation","social media","social network"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-4-D10120BD7B14D1FC30858A70C048E65F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109113511/pnas25urban.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2404.11596","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society","IT University of Copenhagen, MSc in Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"hu","key":"corvinus university of budapest","loc":["Hungary","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Corvinus University of Budapest","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"nl","key":"university of amsterdam","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Amsterdam","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca Maria","key":"aiellopau","last":"Aiello","locid":"6aed9698-9a0c-4956-be0d-ec658bbfe09a","name":"Aiello, Luca Maria","orcid":"0000-0002-0654-2527","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anastassia","key":"vybornovapau","last":"Vybornova","locid":"60b2482e-a0d3-4094-8723-76ea44a33c30","name":"Vybornova, Anastassia","orcid":"0000-0001-6915-2561","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Sándor","key":"juhászpau","last":"Juhász","name":"Juhász, Sándor","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"szellpau","last":"Szell","locid":"2ef641d0-bd79-425b-8b12-b58d74b993ae","name":"Szell, Michael","orcid":"0000-0003-3022-2483","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Eszter","key":"bokányipau","last":"Bokányi","name":"Bokányi, Eszter","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2404.11596","issn":["00278424","10916490"],"issn_org":["00278424"],"issue":"10","journal":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","paperid":"e2408937122","publisher":"The National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","volume":"122"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-86000234747"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ff25188e-1a00-458c-9cc9-495a1baf903e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Urban highways are barriers to social ties","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-808930","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Bicycle infrastructure networks must meet the needs of cyclists to position cycling as a viable transportation choice in cities. In particular, protected infrastructure should be planned cohesively for the whole city and spacious enough to accommodate all cyclists safely and prevent cyclist congestion—a common problem in cycling cities like Copenhagen. Here, we devise an adaptive method for optimal bicycle network design and for evaluating congestion criticalities on bicycle paths. The method goes beyond static network measures, using computationally efficient adaptation rules inspired by optimal transport on the dynamically updating multilayer network of roads and protected bicycle lanes. Street capacities and cyclist flows reciprocally control each other to optimally accommodate cyclists on streets with one control parameter that dictates the preference of bicycle infrastructure over roads. Applying our method to Copenhagen confirms that the city’s bicycle network is generally well-developed. However, we are able to identify the network’s bottlenecks, and we find, at a finer scale, disparities in network accessibility and criticalities between different neighbourhoods. Our model and results are generalizable beyond this particular case study to serve as a scalable and versatile tool for aiding urban planners in designing cycling-friendly cities.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Lonardi, Alessandro","Szell, Michael","De Bacco, Caterina"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-798560"],"filters":{"added":"2025-04","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Max Planck Institute for Intelligent Systems"],"keyword":["Complex systems","Network science","Transportation networks","Urban systems"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"cohesive urban bicycle infrastructure design through optimal transport routing in multilayer networks","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2024.0532","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["The authors thank the International Max Planck Research School for Intelligent Systems (IMPRS-IS) for supporting A.L. M.S. acknowledges funding from EU Horizon Project JUST STREETS (Grant ID: 101104240) and from The Danish Ministry of Transport (Grant ID: CP21-033). All map data from OpenStreetMap."],"id":"S01-000.00-798560","identifier":[{"id":"85217210652","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-d3422dbc-88e1-4a22-a271-6781e343a999"],"keyword":["complex systems","network science","transportation networks","urban systems"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-5F83A37FEDFA108E6BBC208CCCD76637"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2024.0532","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/108679660/lonardi2025cub.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"max planck institute for intelligent systems","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Max Planck Institute for Intelligent Systems","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Climate IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Alessandro","key":"lonardipau","last":"Lonardi","name":"Lonardi, Alessandro","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"szellpau","last":"Szell","locid":"2ef641d0-bd79-425b-8b12-b58d74b993ae","name":"Szell, Michael","orcid":"0000-0003-3022-2483","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Caterina","key":"debaccopau","last":"De Bacco","name":"De Bacco, Caterina","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1098/rsif.2024.0532","issn":["17425662","17425689"],"issn_org":["17425689"],"issue":"223","journal":"Journal of the Royal Society. Interface","pages":"1-12","paperid":"20240532","status":"p","title":"Journal of the Royal Society. Interface","volume":"22"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85217210652"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-d3422dbc-88e1-4a22-a271-6781e343a999"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Cohesive urban bicycle infrastructure design through optimal transport routing in multilayer networks","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-798560","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Walking is the most sustainable form of urban mobility, but is compromised by uncomfortable or unhealthy sun exposure, which is an increasing problem due to global warming. Shade from buildings can provide cooling and protection for pedestrians, but the extent of this potential benefit is unknown. Here we explore the potential for shaded walking, using building footprints and street networks from both synthetic and real cities. We introduce a route choice model with a sun avoidance parameter α and define the CoolWalkability metric to measure opportunities for walking in shade. We derive analytically that on a regular grid with constant building heights, CoolWalkability is independent of α, and that the grid provides no CoolWalkability benefit for shade-seeking individuals compared to the shortest path. However, variations in street geometry and building heights create such benefits. We further uncover that the potential for shaded routing differs between grid-like and irregular street networks, forms local clusters, and is sensitive to the mapped network geometry. Our research identifies the limitations and potential of shade for cool, active travel, and is a first step towards a rigorous understanding of shade provision for sustainable mobility in cities.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Wolf, Henrik","Vierø, Ane Rahbek","Szell, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-818236"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","TU Dresden"],"keyword":["Route choice model","Sun exposure","Urban mobility"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"coolwalks for active mobility in urban street networks","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-97200-2","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["M.Sz. acknowledges funding from EU Horizon Project JUST STREETS (Grant agreement ID: 101104240)"],"id":"S01-000.00-818236","identifier":[{"id":"105003781891","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-42619e77-eb69-452d-aae1-f852b44d6085"],"keyword":["CoolWalkability metric","route choice model","shaded walking","sun exposure","urban mobility"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-C2DE2380C70C2E1CF9BA661C62CFD469"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-97200-2","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"rem","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-97200-2","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"tu dresden","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"TU Dresden","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Climate IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Henrik","key":"wolfpau","last":"Wolf","name":"Wolf, Henrik","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ane Rahbek","key":"vierøpau","last":"Vierø","locid":"45b600aa-d6c7-4c4b-91b3-1d934aec4402","name":"Vierø, Ane Rahbek","orcid":"0000-0002-2404-7271","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"szellpau","last":"Szell","locid":"2ef641d0-bd79-425b-8b12-b58d74b993ae","name":"Szell, Michael","orcid":"0000-0003-3022-2483","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41598-025-97200-2","issn":["20452322"],"journal":"Scientific Reports","paperid":"14911","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group","status":"p","title":"Scientific Reports","volume":"15"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105003781891"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-42619e77-eb69-452d-aae1-f852b44d6085"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"CoolWalks for active mobility in urban street networks","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-818236","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"In this chapter, we discuss urban mobility from a complexity science perspective. First, we give an overview of the datasets that enable this approach, such as mobile phone records, location-based social network traces, or GPS trajectories from sensors installed on vehicles. We then review the empirical and theoretical understanding of the properties of human movements, including the distribution of travel distances and times, the entropy of trajectories, and the interplay between exploration and exploitation of locations. Next, we explain generative and predictive models of individual mobility, and their limitations due to intrinsic limits of predictability. Finally, we discuss urban transport from a systemic perspective, including system-wide challenges like ridesharing, multimodality, and sustainable transport.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Alessandretti, Laura","Szell, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-828117"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Szell, Michael"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"urban mobility","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105010759370","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-828117","identifier":[{"id":"105010759370","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-f9d5ec1d-6b56-40a1-8bcc-d649cc19eed4"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-46317BC1721F2D3437C89B67EF05595F"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science, Cognitive Systems"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Laura","key":"alessandrettipau","last":"Alessandretti","locid":"0e9909a3-464d-4dbe-8e3a-05327a3bc245","name":"Alessandretti, Laura","orcid":"0000-0001-6003-1165","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"szellpau","last":"Szell","name":"Szell, Michael","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-031-82666-5_4","eisbn":["9783031826665"],"isbn":["9783031826658"],"issn":["18600840"],"pages":"75-98","publisher":"Springer","series":"Understanding Complex Systems","status":"p","title":"Compendium of Urban Complexity","volume":"560"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105010759370"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-f9d5ec1d-6b56-40a1-8bcc-d649cc19eed4"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Urban Mobility","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-828117","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Spatial data science is an emerging field building on geographic information science, geography, and data science. Here we first discuss the definition and history of the field, arguing that it indeed warrants a new label. Then, we present the design of our course Geospatial Data Science at IT University of Copenhagen and discuss the importance of teaching not just spatial data science tools but also spatial and critical thinking. We conclude with a perspective on the potential future for spatial data science, arguing that qualitative theory and methods will continue to play an important role despite new GeoAI-related advances. ‘Spatial data science’ er et spirende felt, der udspringer af både geografisk informationsvidenskab, geografi og data science. I denne artikel diskuterer vi først begrebet spatial data science, introducerer dets historie og argumenterer for, at det repræsenterer et nybrud i forhold til tidligere tendenser indenfor GIS og geodata. Herefter præsenterer vi vores overvejelser om undervisning i spatial data science i forbindelse med udviklingen af kurset Geospatial Data Science på IT Universitet i København. Vi understreger vigtigheden i ikke bare at undervise i praktiske anvendelser af geodata, men også at lære de studerende geografisk og kritisk tænkning. Afslutningsvis diskuterer vi fremtiden for spatial data science, og argumenterer for, at geografisk teori og metoder vil forblive relevante trods af den stigende indflydelse fra GeoAI.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Vierø, Ane Rahbek","Szell, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-789551"],"filters":{"added":"2025-02","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Data science","GeoAI","Python","Spatial data science","Teaching"],"level":"pop","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"teaching spatial data science","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.54337/ojs.perspektiv.v23i44.8383","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-789551","ids":["itu-53ec5342-d7d8-40d7-a12f-ffea725c870d"],"keyword":["Data science","GeoAI","Python","Teaching","spatial data science"],"lang":"English","level":"Popular","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-73C28C02F16D3E44D81CA6038A6757CF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.54337/ojs.perspektiv.v23i44.8383","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ane Rahbek","key":"vierøpau","last":"Vierø","locid":"45b600aa-d6c7-4c4b-91b3-1d934aec4402","name":"Vierø, Ane Rahbek","orcid":"0000-0002-2404-7271","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"szellpau","last":"Szell","locid":"2ef641d0-bd79-425b-8b12-b58d74b993ae","name":"Szell, Michael","orcid":"0000-0003-3022-2483","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"]},"sid":["itu-53ec5342-d7d8-40d7-a12f-ffea725c870d"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Teaching spatial data science","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-789551","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Cycling is a key ingredient for a sustainability shift of Denmark's transportation system. To increase cycling rates, better bicycle infrastructure networks are required. Planning such networks requires high-quality infrastructure data, yet the quality of bicycle infrastructure data is understudied. Here, we compare the two largest open data sets on dedicated bicycle infrastructure in Denmark, OpenStreetMap (OSM) and GeoDanmark, in a countrywide data quality assessment, asking whether the data are good enough for network-based analysis of cycling conditions. We find that neither of the data sets is of sufficient quality, and that data conflation is necessary to obtain a more complete data set. Our analysis of the spatial variation of data quality suggests that rural areas are more prone to incomplete data. We demonstrate that the prevalent method of using infrastructure density as a proxy for data completeness is not suitable for bicycle infrastructure data, and that matching of corresponding features is thus necessary to assess data completeness. Based on our data quality assessment, we recommend strategic mapping efforts toward data completeness, consistent standards to support comparability between different data sources, and increased focus on data topology to ensure high-quality bicycle network data.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Vierø, Ane Rahbek","Vybornova, Anastassia","Szell, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-730156"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Bicycle infrastructure","Bicycle networks","Open data","OpenStreetMap","Spatial data quality"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"how good is open bicycle network data a countrywide case study of denmark","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"rem","url":"http://michael.szell.net/downloads/viero2024hgo.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Danish Ministry of Transport. Grant Number: CP21-033"],"id":"S01-000.00-730156","identifier":[{"id":"85189989316","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-00865963-6ad8-41ef-a860-5258d1c67b74"],"keyword":["Bicycle infrastructure","Bicycle networks","Open data","OpenStreetMap","Spatial data quality"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-911D040BFB319479D9E23F2E9AD6FDA1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"rem","url":"http://michael.szell.net/downloads/viero2024hgo.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Climate IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ane Rahbek","key":"vierøpau","last":"Vierø","locid":"45b600aa-d6c7-4c4b-91b3-1d934aec4402","name":"Vierø, Ane Rahbek","orcid":"0000-0002-2404-7271","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anastassia","key":"vybornovapau","last":"Vybornova","locid":"60b2482e-a0d3-4094-8723-76ea44a33c30","name":"Vybornova, Anastassia","orcid":"0000-0001-6915-2561","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"szellpau","last":"Szell","locid":"2ef641d0-bd79-425b-8b12-b58d74b993ae","name":"Szell, Michael","orcid":"0000-0003-3022-2483","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/gean.12400","issn":["15384632"],"journal":"Geographical Analysis","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell","status":"p","title":"Geographical Analysis"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85189989316"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-00865963-6ad8-41ef-a860-5258d1c67b74"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"How Good Is Open Bicycle Network Data? A Countrywide Case Study of Denmark","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-730156","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"superblockify is a Python package designed to assist in planning future Superblock implementations by partitioning an urban street network into Superblock-like neighborhoods and providing tools for visualizing and analyzing these partition results. A Superblock is a set of adjacent urban blocks where vehicular through traffic is prevented or pacified, giving priority to people walking and cycling (Nieuwenhuijsen et al., 2024). The potential Superblock blueprints and descriptive statistics generated by superblockify can be used by urban planners as a first step in a data-driven planning pipeline for future urban transformations, or by urban data scientists as an efficient computational method to evaluate potential Superblock partitions. The software is licensed under AGPLv3 and is available at https://superblockify.city.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Büth, Carlson Moses","Vybornova, Anastassia","Szell, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-754075"],"filters":{"added":"2024-09","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Münster"],"keyword":["Geospatial analysis","Network analysis","Urban mobility","Urban planning"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"superblockify a python package for automated generation visualization and analysis of potential superblocks in cities","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.21105/joss.06798","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["EU Horizon Project JUST STREETS (Grant agreement ID: 101104240)"],"id":"S01-000.00-754075","ids":["itu-74363520-b1a3-4e2f-bfa8-efca152b64d0"],"keyword":["geospatial analysis","network analysis","urban mobility","urban planning"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-1B8807326569B05B1F09DEA1660DE050"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.21105/joss.06798","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"university of münster","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Münster","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Climate IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Carlson Moses","key":"büthpau","last":"Büth","name":"Büth, Carlson Moses","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Anastassia","key":"vybornovapau","last":"Vybornova","locid":"60b2482e-a0d3-4094-8723-76ea44a33c30","name":"Vybornova, Anastassia","orcid":"0000-0001-6915-2561","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"szellpau","last":"Szell","locid":"2ef641d0-bd79-425b-8b12-b58d74b993ae","name":"Szell, Michael","orcid":"0000-0003-3022-2483","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.21105/joss.06798","issn":["24759066"],"issue":"100","journal":"The Journal of Open Source Software","paperid":"6798","status":"p","title":"The Journal of Open Source Software","title_org":"Journal of Open Source Software","volume":"9"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-74363520-b1a3-4e2f-bfa8-efca152b64d0"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"superblockify: A Python Package for Automated Generation, Visualization, and Analysis of Potential Superblocks in Cities","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-754075","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"The Physics Nobel Prize was awarded to the groundbreaking research of John Hopfield and Geoffrey Hinton on artificial neural networks. This surprised many scientists and caused dissenting opinions, as this topic is commonly perceived as Computer Science - So why the prize in Physics? Instead of relying on opinions, there are robust, data-driven methods from Science of Science to contextualize this award using citation data. This way, we have shown that indeed ``Hopfield's 1982 paper on neural networks [is] indistinguishable from papers published in physics journals'', similar to ``six physics Nobel winning publications'' in interdisciplinary physics. Further, we assessed that interdisciplinary papers like Hopfield's and Hinton's are ripe for a Nobel Prize: Until recently, the Physics Nobel Prize has been used to award traditional physics research impacting physics only; this year's award however shows that the prize has caught up with reality, recognizing interdisciplinary discoveries that impact both physics and other fields. We are hopeful that this recognition will expedite the breakup of disciplinary silos which obstruct out-of-the-box thinking that combines ideas from different disciplines. This breakup is urgently needed to solve the world's big challenges like climate change. Physics itself is undergoing extensive changes and ``spin-offs'', influencing emerging fields like data science that embrace interdisciplinarity. In contrast, clinging to research fields as fixed territories is at best small-minded, at worst harmful. Although we should not discard useful domain knowledge, there is a clear need for better use of data and breaking up silo mentalities.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Szell, Michael","Ma, Yifang","Sinatra, Roberta"],"cuid":["S02-085.09-804389"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Asia, East","OECD"],"country":["China","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Northwestern University","Southern University of Science and Technology"],"keyword":["Machine learning","Physics","Scientific community"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci","soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"was the nobel prize for physics yes not that it matters","src":["itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/107042248/szell2024wnp.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S02-085.09-804389","identifier":[{"id":"001344642400002","type":"wos"},{"id":"85207502283","type":"scopus"},{"id":"39438749","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["itu-12c76d0b-1461-4612-b998-c55df87095b5","ku-bfac9f0f-d7a9-4b98-9516-3475a8988ab9"],"keyword":["Machine learning","Physics","Scientific community"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology","Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-BE7ABFCE3BEFDE2E986E4ED139FF5606"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/107042248/szell2024wnp.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"northwestern university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Northwestern University","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"cn","key":"southern university of science and technology","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Southern University of Science and Technology","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, SODAS, SODAS"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"szellpau","last":"Szell","locid":"2ef641d0-bd79-425b-8b12-b58d74b993ae","name":"Szell, Michael","orcid":"0000-0003-3022-2483","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Yifang","key":"mapau","last":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yifang","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Roberta","key":"sinatrapau","last":"Sinatra","locid":"418f030f-2b26-4c74-a77b-c0c87d13f56d","name":"Sinatra, Roberta","orcid":"0000-0002-7558-1028","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["39438749"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/d41586-024-03435-w","issn":["00280836","14764687"],"issn_org":["00280836"],"issue":"8035","journal":"Nature","pages":"782-782","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group","status":"p","title":"Nature","volume":"634"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-12c76d0b-1461-4612-b998-c55df87095b5","ku-bfac9f0f-d7a9-4b98-9516-3475a8988ab9"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Was the Nobel prize for physics? Yes - not that it matters","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-085.09-804389","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Transportation networks, from bicycle paths to buses and railways, are the backbone of urban mobility. In large metropolitan areas, the integration of different transport modes has become crucial to guarantee the fast and sustainable flow of people. Using a network science approach, multimodal transport systems can be described as multilayer networks, where the networks associated to different transport modes are not considered in isolation, but as a set of interconnected layers. Despite the importance of multimodality in modern cities, a unified view of the topic is currently missing. Here, we provide a comprehensive overview of the emerging research areas of multilayer transport networks and multimodal urban mobility, focusing on contributions from the interdisciplinary fields of complex systems, urban data science, and science of cities. First, we present an introduction to the mathematical framework of multilayer networks. We apply it to survey models of multimodal infrastructures, as well as measures used for quantifying multimodality, and related empirical findings. We review modeling approaches and observational evidence in multimodal mobility and public transport system dynamics, focusing on integrated real-world mobility patterns, where individuals navigate urban systems using different transport modes. We then provide a survey of freely available datasets on multimodal infrastructure and mobility, and a list of open-source tools for their analyses. Finally, we conclude with an outlook on open research questions and promising directions for future research.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Alessandretti, Laura","Natera Orozco, Luis Guillermo","Battiston, Federico","Saberi, Meead","Szell, Michael"],"cuid":["S02-088.91-704446"],"filters":{"added":"2024-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD","Oceania"],"corresponding_author":["Alessandretti, Laura"],"country":["Australia","Hungary","Italy"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Central European University","Institute for Scientific Interchange Foundation","University of New South Wales"],"keyword":["Complex systems","Human mobility","Multilayer networks","Science of cities","Transport networks","Urban data science"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"multimodal urban mobility and multilayer transport networks","src":["dtu","itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/283411114/2111.02152.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S02-088.91-704446","identifier":[{"id":"85134607865","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-1a2e9684-58ae-48d6-9db1-a3cbcb2e5bb2","itu-091513bd-fb26-4838-ad2d-51ce2833a6ae"],"keyword":["Complex systems","Human mobility","Multilayer networks","Science of cities","Transport networks","Urban data science"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-CB7EE78C7078B8DBA962930F0F4B8DAB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86905843/alessandretti2022mum.pdf","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/283411114/2111.02152.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science, Cognitive Systems"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"hu","key":"central european university","loc":["Hungary","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Central European University","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"au","key":"university of new south wales","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"University of New South Wales","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Laura","key":"alessandrettipau","last":"Alessandretti","locid":"0e9909a3-464d-4dbe-8e3a-05327a3bc245","name":"Alessandretti, Laura","orcid":"0000-0001-6003-1165","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Luis Guillermo","key":"nateraorozcopau","last":"Natera Orozco","name":"Natera Orozco, Luis Guillermo","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Federico","key":"battistonpau","last":"Battiston","name":"Battiston, Federico","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"szellpau","last":"Szell","locid":"2ef641d0-bd79-425b-8b12-b58d74b993ae","name":"Szell, Michael","orcid":"0000-0003-3022-2483","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Meead","key":"saberipau","last":"Saberi","name":"Saberi, Meead","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/23998083221108190","issn":["23998083","23998091"],"issn_org":["23998083"],"issue":"8","journal":"Environment and Planning B: Urban Analytics and City Science","pages":"2038-2070","publisher":"SAGE Publications","status":"p","title":"Environment and Planning B: Urban Analytics and City Science","volume":"50"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85134607865"],"sdg":["11. Sustainable Cities and Production","All UN SDG Classification"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-1a2e9684-58ae-48d6-9db1-a3cbcb2e5bb2","itu-091513bd-fb26-4838-ad2d-51ce2833a6ae"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Multimodal urban mobility and multilayer transport networks","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-088.91-704446","year":2023,"yearsub":2023}
{"abstract":"From a transport perspective, increasing active travel –and walking in particular– is crucial for the future of sustainable cities, as reflected in global decarbonisation policies and agendas. Further, walking is much more than a mere mode of transport: it provides a fundamental social function, fostering vibrant cohesive communities. Arguably, walking and its associated infrastructure –sidewalks– should rank among the highest priorities for planning authorities. However, efficiency- and speed-driven urbanisation has gradually reallocated street space to private cars, leading to automobiles being the prioritised mode of transport today. Empirical research has generally followed suit, and a systemic understanding of walking as a phenomenon is largely missing, i.e., questions like how connected, resilient, accessible, or socially equitable is the pedestrian infrastructure of whole neighbourhoods and cities. Such relative neglect of sidewalk network research is, first and foremost, the consequence of a generalised lack of publicly available data on sidewalk infrastructure worldwide. A second reason might be its apparent lack of interest from a systemic standpoint: pedestrian mobility does not produce coordination challenges on the scale that cars do. In this work, we confront this perception by showing that there is ample research potential in the study of system-wide sidewalk networks, with both structural and dynamical challenges which might be critical to pursue the latest aspirations towards sustainable mobility in cities.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Rhoads, Daniel","Rames, Clément","Solé-Ribalta, Albert","González, Marta","Szell, Michael","Borge-Holthoefer, Javier"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-695273"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Spain","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Open University of Catalonia","University of California"],"keyword":["Accessibility","Multilayer networks","Pedestrian dynamics","Sidewalk network","Urban complex systems"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"sidewalk networks review and outlook","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.compenvurbsys.2023.102031","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-695273","identifier":[{"id":"85171457102","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-9e9d3983-a86b-4efd-9ccd-3d4c3a63deab"],"keyword":["Accessibility","Multilayer networks","Pedestrian dynamics","Sidewalk network","Urban complex systems"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-7CF9EFBB0289F77300ABC1F8F080DB2D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101625087/rhoads2023snr.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.compenvurbsys.2023.102031","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"es","key":"open university of catalonia","loc":["Spain","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Open University of Catalonia","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"university of california","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of California","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Climate IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniel","key":"rhoadspau","last":"Rhoads","name":"Rhoads, Daniel","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Clément","key":"ramespau","last":"Rames","name":"Rames, Clément","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Albert","key":"soléribaltapau","last":"Solé-Ribalta","name":"Solé-Ribalta, Albert","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Marta","key":"gonzálezpau","last":"González","name":"González, Marta","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"szellpau","last":"Szell","locid":"2ef641d0-bd79-425b-8b12-b58d74b993ae","name":"Szell, Michael","orcid":"0000-0003-3022-2483","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Javier","key":"borgeholthoeferpau","last":"Borge-Holthoefer","name":"Borge-Holthoefer, Javier","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.compenvurbsys.2023.102031","issn":["01989715","18737587"],"journal":"Computers, Environment and Urban Systems","pages":"102031","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Computers, Environment and Urban Systems","volume":"106"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85171457102"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-9e9d3983-a86b-4efd-9ccd-3d4c3a63deab"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Sidewalk networks: Review and outlook","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-695273","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"High-quality data on existing bicycle infrastructure are a requirement for evidence-based bicycle network planning, which supports a green transition of human mobility. However, this requirement is rarely met: Data from governmental agencies or crowdsourced projects like OpenStreetMap often suffer from unknown, heterogeneous, or low quality. Currently available tools for road network data quality assessment often fail to account for network topology, spatial heterogeneity, and bicycle-specific data characteristics. To fill these gaps, we introduce BikeDNA, an open-source tool for reproducible quality assessment tailored to bicycle infrastructure data with a focus on network structure and connectivity. BikeDNA performs either a standalone analysis of one data set or a comparative analysis between OpenStreetMap and a reference data set, including feature matching. Data quality metrics are considered both globally for the entire study area and locally on grid cell level, thus exposing spatial variation in data quality. Interactive maps and HTML/PDF reports are generated to facilitate the visual exploration and communication of results. BikeDNA supports quality assessments of bicycle infrastructure data for a wide range of applications -- from urban planning to OpenStreetMap data improvement or network research for sustainable mobility.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Vierø, Ane Rahbek","Vybornova, Anastassia","Szell, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-695240"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Bicycle networks","Open source software","OpenStreetMap","Spatial data quality","Volunteered Geographic Information"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"bikedna a tool for bicycle infrastructure data and network assessment","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"https://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/license.html","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2303.01223","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-695240","identifier":[{"id":"85162649377","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-44371a4f-0e57-4472-a28d-c4e445a6c87b"],"keyword":["OpenStreetMap","bicycle networks","open-source software","spatial data quality","volunteered geographic information"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-5F5FCA5C4375303628AC6A4E23BEEE6D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"https://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/license.html","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2303.01223","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Climate IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ane Rahbek","key":"vierøpau","last":"Vierø","locid":"45b600aa-d6c7-4c4b-91b3-1d934aec4402","name":"Vierø, Ane Rahbek","orcid":"0000-0002-2404-7271","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anastassia","key":"vybornovapau","last":"Vybornova","locid":"60b2482e-a0d3-4094-8723-76ea44a33c30","name":"Vybornova, Anastassia","orcid":"0000-0001-6915-2561","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"szellpau","last":"Szell","locid":"2ef641d0-bd79-425b-8b12-b58d74b993ae","name":"Szell, Michael","orcid":"0000-0003-3022-2483","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/23998083231184471","issn":["23998083","23998091"],"journal":"Environment and Planning B: Urban Analytics and City Science","publisher":"Sage Publishing","status":"p","title":"Environment and Planning B: Urban Analytics and City Science"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85162649377"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-44371a4f-0e57-4472-a28d-c4e445a6c87b"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"BikeDNA: A tool for bicycle infrastructure data and network assessment","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-695240","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Cycling is an effective solution for making urban transport more sustainable. However, bicycle networks are typically developed in a slow, piecewise process that leaves open a large number of gaps, even in well-developed cycling cities like Copenhagen. Here, we develop the IPDC procedure (Identify, Prioritize, Decluster, Classify) for finding the most important missing links in urban bicycle networks, using data from OpenStreetMap. In this procedure we first identify all possible gaps following a multiplex network approach, prioritize them according to a flow-based metric, decluster emerging gap clusters, and manually classify the types of gaps. We apply the IPDC procedure to Copenhagen and report the 105 top priority gaps. For evaluation, we compare these gaps with the city’s most recent Cycle Path Prioritization Plan and find considerable overlaps. Our results show how network analysis with minimal data requirements can serve as a cost-efficient support tool for bicycle network planning. By taking into account the whole city network for consolidating urban bicycle infrastructure, our data-driven framework can complement localized, manual planning processes for more effective, city-wide decision-making.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Vybornova, Anastassia","Cunha, Tiago Oliveira","Gühnemann, Astrid","Szell, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382962"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Austria"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Universität für Bodenkultur Wien"],"keyword":["Network analysis","OpenStreetMap","Sustainable mobility","Urban data science","Urban planning","bicycle network"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"automated detection of missing links in bicycle networks","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86882136/vybornova_bikenwgaps_main.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382962","identifier":[{"id":"85126773622","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-728aafa1-8e3a-45d2-800c-8e82aac01a41"],"keyword":["OpenStreetMap","bicycle network","network analysis","sustainable mobility","urban data science","urban planning"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-8DF49F92DCF69FA33E01F1F9A6626F5B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86882136/vybornova_bikenwgaps_main.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"at","key":"universität für bodenkultur wien","loc":["Austria","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Universität für Bodenkultur Wien","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anastassia","key":"vybornovapau","last":"Vybornova","locid":"60b2482e-a0d3-4094-8723-76ea44a33c30","name":"Vybornova, Anastassia","orcid":"0000-0001-6915-2561","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Tiago Oliveira","key":"cunhapau","last":"Cunha","name":"Cunha, Tiago Oliveira","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Astrid","key":"gühnemannpau","last":"Gühnemann","name":"Gühnemann, Astrid","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"szellpau","last":"Szell","locid":"2ef641d0-bd79-425b-8b12-b58d74b993ae","name":"Szell, Michael","orcid":"0000-0003-3022-2483","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/gean.12324","issn":["15384632"],"journal":"Geographical Analysis","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell","status":"p","title":"Geographical Analysis"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85126773622"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-728aafa1-8e3a-45d2-800c-8e82aac01a41"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Automated Detection of Missing Links in Bicycle Networks","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382962","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"One of the targets of the UN Sustainable Development Goals is to substantially reduce the number of global deaths and injuries from road traffic collisions. To this aim, European cities adopted various urban mobility policies, which has led to a heterogeneous number of injuries across Europe. Monitoring the discrepancies in injuries and understanding the most efficient policies are keys to achieve the objectives of Vision Zero, a multi-national road traffic safety project that aims at zero fatalities or serious injuries linked to road traffic. Here, we identify urban features that are determinants of vulnerable road user safety through the analysis of inter-mode collision data across European cities. We first build up a data set of urban road crashes and their participants from 24 cities in 5 European countries, using the widely recommended KSI indicator (killed or seriously injured individuals) as a safety performance metric. Modelling the casualty matrices including road infrastructure characteristics and modal share distribution of the different cities, we observe that cities with the highest rates of walking and cycling modal shares are the safest for the most vulnerable users. Instead, a higher presence of low-speed limited roads seems to only significantly reduce the number of injuries of car occupants. Our results suggest that policies aimed at increasing the modal share of walking and cycling are key to improve road safety for all road users.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Klanjcic, Marina","Gauvin, Laetitia","Tizzoni, Michele","Szell, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382569"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Italy"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Institute for Scientific Interchange Foundation"],"keyword":["Casualty matrix","Mobility","Roadsafety","Urbandatascience"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"identifying urban features for vulnerable road user safety in europe","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1140/epjds/s13688-022-00339-5","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382569","identifier":[{"id":"85128105527","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-57aff91f-239c-45c1-ace2-8a6129a80ffa"],"keyword":["Casualty matrix","Mobility","Roadsafety","Urbandatascience"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-5088B23DE8C09173918334DE36765FBF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86723278/klanjcic2022iuf.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1140/epjds/s13688-022-00339-5","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"it","key":"institute for scientific interchange foundation","loc":["Italy","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Institute for Scientific Interchange Foundation","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marina","key":"klanjcicpau","last":"Klanjcic","name":"Klanjcic, Marina","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Laetitia","key":"gauvinpau","last":"Gauvin","name