{"abstract":"Urban mobility increasingly relies on multimodality, combining the use of bicycle paths, streets, and rail networks. These different modes of transportation are well described by multiplex networks. Here we propose the overlap census method which extracts a multimodal profile from a city’s multiplex transportation network. We apply this method to 15 cities, identify clusters of cities with similar profiles, and link this feature to the level of sustainable mobility of each cluster. Our work highlights the importance of evaluating all the transportation systems of a city together to adequately identify and compare its potential for sustainable, multimodal mobility.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Natera Orozco, Luis Guillermo","Battiston, Federico","Iñiguez, Gerardo","Szell, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384229"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Hungary"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Central European University"],"keyword":["Multiplex networks","Sustainable transport","multi modal transportation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"extracting the multimodal fingerprint of urban transportation networks","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-SA","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.32866/001c.13171","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384229","identifier":[{"id":"85094770401","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-ca370155-c92a-424e-be89-38e1897affea"],"keyword":["multi-modal transportation","multiplex networks","sustainable transport"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-7EBC65511D8F8552704442DA02A61ACD"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-SA","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85284782/nateraorozco2020emf.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-SA","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.32866/001c.13171","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"hu","key":"central european university","loc":["Hungary","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Central European University","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luis Guillermo","key":"nateraorozcopau","last":"Natera Orozco","name":"Natera Orozco, Luis Guillermo","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Federico","key":"battistonpau","last":"Battiston","name":"Battiston, Federico","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Gerardo","key":"iñiguezpau","last":"Iñiguez","name":"Iñiguez, Gerardo","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"szellpau","last":"Szell","locid":"2ef641d0-bd79-425b-8b12-b58d74b993ae","name":"Szell, Michael","orcid":"0000-0003-3022-2483","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.32866/001c.13171","issn":["26528800"],"journal":"Transport Findings","paperid":"13171","status":"p","title":"Transport Findings"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ca370155-c92a-424e-be89-38e1897affea"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Extracting the multimodal fingerprint of urban transportation networks","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384229","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Urban transportation networks, from pavements and bicycle paths to streets and railways, provide the backbone for movement and socioeconomic life in cities. To make urban transport sustainable, cities are increasingly investing to develop their bicycle networks. However, it is yet unclear how to extend them comprehensively and effectively given a limited budget. Here we investigate the structure of bicycle networks in cities around the world, and find that they consist of hundreds of disconnected patches, even in cycling-friendly cities like Copenhagen. To connect these patches, we develop and apply data-driven, algorithmic network growth strategies, showing that small but focused investments allow to significantly increase the connectedness and directness of urban bicycle networks. We introduce two greedy algorithms to add the most critical missing links in the bicycle network focusing on connectedness, and show that they outmatch both a random approach and a baseline minimum investment strategy. Our computational approach outlines novel pathways from car-centric towards sustainable cities by taking advantage of urban data available on a city-wide scale. It is a first step towards a quantitative consolidation of bicycle infrastructure development that can become valuable for urban planners and stakeholders.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Natera Orozco, Luis Guillermo","Battiston, Federico","Iñiguez, Gerardo","Szell, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384666"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Hungary"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Central European University"],"keyword":["Bicycle infrastructure","Sustainable transport","network growth"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"data driven strategies for optimal bicycle network growth","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.201130","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384666","identifier":[{"id":"85099588457","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-e6a27962-b104-4e15-bdb5-5f4fb2a474ba"],"keyword":["bicycle infrastructure","network growth","sustainable transport"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-9B63E7E85B9A907A1606125B9763F34A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.201130","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85582532/nateraorozco2020dso.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"hu","key":"central european university","loc":["Hungary","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Central European University","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luis Guillermo","key":"nateraorozcopau","last":"Natera Orozco","name":"Natera Orozco, Luis Guillermo","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Federico","key":"battistonpau","last":"Battiston","name":"Battiston, Federico","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Gerardo","key":"iñiguezpau","last":"Iñiguez","name":"Iñiguez, Gerardo","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"szellpau","last":"Szell","locid":"2ef641d0-bd79-425b-8b12-b58d74b993ae","name":"Szell, Michael","orcid":"0000-0003-3022-2483","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1098/rsos.201130","issn":["20545703"],"issue":"201130","journal":"Royal Society Open Science","paperid":"201130","status":"p","title":"Royal Society Open Science","volume":"7"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85099588457"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-e6a27962-b104-4e15-bdb5-5f4fb2a474ba"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Data-driven strategies for optimal bicycle network growth","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384666","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Due to the wide-spread use of disruptive digital technologies like mobile phones, cities have transitioned from data-scarce to data-rich environments. As a result, the field of geoinformatics is being reshaped and challenged to develop adequate data-driven methods. At the same time, the term \"smart city\" is increasingly being applied in urban planning, reflecting the aims of different stakeholders to create value out of the new data sets. However, many smart city research initiatives are promoting techno-positivistic approaches which do not account enough for the citizens’ needs. In this paper, we review the state of quantitative urban studies under this new perspective, and critically discuss the development of smart city programs. We conclude with a call for a new anti-disciplinary, human-centric urban data science, and a well-reflected use of technology and data collection in smart city planning. Finally, we introduce the papers of this special issue which focus on providing a more human-centric view on data-driven urban studies, spanning topics from cycling and wellbeing, to mobility and land use.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Resch, Bernd","Szell, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382530"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Austria"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Paris-Lodron University of Salzburg"],"keyword":["Smart cities","Urban data science","geoinformatics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"human centric data science for urban studies","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dje","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/ijgi8120584","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382530","identifier":[{"id":"85076702600","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-544e5cfe-7062-4fbd-8218-7ec68d4a8fd3"],"keyword":["geoinformatics","smart cities","urban data science"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-329ABEBEAE5C1E81EC307BB354733D7E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/2220-9964/8/12/584","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/ijgi8120584","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"at","key":"paris lodron university of salzburg","loc":["Austria","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Paris-Lodron University of Salzburg","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bernd","key":"reschpau","last":"Resch","name":"Resch, Bernd","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"szellpau","last":"Szell","locid":"2ef641d0-bd79-425b-8b12-b58d74b993ae","name":"Szell, Michael","orcid":"0000-0003-3022-2483","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.3390/ijgi8120584","issn":["22209964"],"journal":"ISPRS International Journal of Geo-Information","pages":"584","paperid":"8","publisher":"MDPI AG","publisher_org":"MDPIAG","status":"p","title":"ISPRS International Journal of Geo-Information","volume":"8"},"review":"No Review","scoID":["2-s2.0-85076702600"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-544e5cfe-7062-4fbd-8218-7ec68d4a8fd3"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Human-Centric Data Science for Urban Studies","type":"Editorial","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382530","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
